#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "9f2942ee-4d9f-5436-a021-9e325bfc694b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10450" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $16$ and $6$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then:\n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1$,\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C$, in descending order, are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=16\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=6\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=6{:}\n$$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC - 6I &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix} - 6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(C-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second principal component of the data is $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "1ffdc04e-8224-542d-80f5-99322b693ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9836" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $S$ are $8$, $5$, and $2$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then:\n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1$,\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C$, in descending order, are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=8,\\qquad \\lambda_2=5,\\qquad \\lambda_3=2\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=8{:}\n$$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC - 8I &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(C-8I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| =3\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first principal component of the data is $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923e5f47-7c27-5b25-99d0-faddfb3a1c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189677} {:answer/id #uuid "d49bd9e8-39db-5a09-9bd4-9d3c42561a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189678} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fefe99-0668-5c25-be0f-416c2ea8a797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189679} {:answer/id #uuid "1447777a-21d5-5f47-9dbc-8d24999a8b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189680} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa942f5-62e2-5bf3-8d52-255bca8240ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189681}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebf47f9-948f-5a15-bd99-d2bd7bdca3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189676}] :question/id #uuid "bff16bd8-7b45-5bbd-8528-7bc476ccdc82" :question/math-academy-id "q-137740" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below.\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10$, $5$, and $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ff0ac0d-7894-5cf4-86b4-3e48ee737219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189689} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0f1a6d-84e8-5b44-bf0c-0cb4cb656d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189690} {:answer/id #uuid "f58626a7-2345-58d4-a30b-d2df61b691f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189691} {:answer/id #uuid "44f775b4-228e-5e4c-859c-ca25db3d9731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189692} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc8bea4-4f2c-5864-a526-b54d33a8792f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189693}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e265595c-4439-5c82-a9e6-7d73e24470a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 175921861